1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,671
When a visitor follows a broken link, or a signed-in user lacks permission

2
00:00:05,671 --> 00:00:08,415
for a page, the site shows an error page.

3
00:00:08,415 --> 00:00:12,093
The Orchard Diagnostics feature draws it from a template.

4
00:00:12,093 --> 00:00:15,830
In this video, you replace the Not Found page in two ways.

5
00:00:20,430 --> 00:00:24,449
When a request ends with an error status, such as 404, the Orchard

6
00:00:24,449 --> 00:00:28,523
Diagnostics feature runs the request again against the Error route.

7
00:00:28,523 --> 00:00:31,370
The status code is the last part of the path.

8
00:00:32,120 --> 00:00:36,405
The Error action builds a shape of type HttpError.

9
00:00:36,405 --> 00:00:42,526
For a 404, it adds two alternates, which are more specific template names:

10
00:00:42,526 --> 00:00:49,260
HttpError__404 for the number, and HttpError__NotFound for the name of the status.

11
00:00:50,010 --> 00:00:57,127
The module has a view for BadRequest, Unauthorized, Forbidden, NotFound and TooManyRequests.

12
00:00:57,127 --> 00:01:03,299
Every other status uses the fallback view, HttpError, unless you add a template

13
00:01:03,299 --> 00:01:06,930
for it, such as HttpError__MethodNotAllowed.

14
00:01:11,159 --> 00:01:13,646
Let's request a page that does not exist.

15
00:01:13,646 --> 00:01:17,880
The site answers with the Not Found page of the Diagnostics feature, drawn

16
00:01:17,880 --> 00:01:20,209
inside the layout of the active theme.

17
00:01:20,684 --> 00:01:25,156
To preview another status, open the Error route with its code.

18
00:01:25,156 --> 00:01:27,918
For 403, the Forbidden page appears.

19
00:01:27,918 --> 00:01:31,864
The Users module uses this page when access is denied.

20
00:01:32,327 --> 00:01:38,069
A status without its own view, such as 405, shows the fallback view, which reads:

21
00:01:38,069 --> 00:01:41,567
An error occurred while executing this request.

22
00:01:45,767 --> 00:01:52,909
To replace the page in a theme, add a view named HttpError-NotFound.cshtml to its Views folder.

23
00:01:52,909 --> 00:01:58,777
The hyphen in the file name stands for the two underscores of the shape name.

24
00:01:59,527 --> 00:02:04,419
A Liquid view takes the same place, when the Liquid feature is enabled.

25
00:02:04,419 --> 00:02:09,120
Name it HttpError-NotFound.liquid, and write the markup with Liquid.

26
00:02:09,120 --> 00:02:13,947
The t filter passes the text to localization, so it can be translated.

27
00:02:14,697 --> 00:02:17,406
The fallback view is HttpError.

28
00:02:17,406 --> 00:02:24,360
Its model, HttpErrorShapeViewModel, holds the Code and the HttpStatusCode of the request.

29
00:02:24,360 --> 00:02:29,117
Both are empty when an unhandled exception led to the page.

30
00:02:33,334 --> 00:02:37,569
You can also replace the page from the admin, without changing a theme.

31
00:02:37,569 --> 00:02:42,794
With the Templates feature enabled, open Design, then Templates, and select Add Template.

32
00:02:43,267 --> 00:02:48,990
The name of a template is the name of the shape, so let's type HttpError__NotFound.

33
00:02:48,990 --> 00:02:52,077
The Content field takes a Liquid template.

34
00:02:52,527 --> 00:02:56,227
Let's write a heading, and pass its text through the t filter so

35
00:02:56,227 --> 00:02:57,654
it can be translated.

36
00:02:57,654 --> 00:03:00,877
Then select Save, and the template appears in the list.

37
00:03:01,337 --> 00:03:03,565
Let's request the missing page again.

38
00:03:03,565 --> 00:03:06,517
It shows your heading in place of the built-in one.

39
00:03:08,117 --> 00:03:12,131
The Templates recipe step creates templates when a recipe runs.

40
00:03:12,131 --> 00:03:15,854
It maps each template name to its Description and Content.

41
00:03:15,854 --> 00:03:21,147
The All Templates deployment step exports the templates of a site in the same format.

42
00:03:25,347 --> 00:03:29,475
For a status, the names are tried in a fixed order:

43
00:03:29,475 --> 00:03:33,965
the name of the status, then the number, then HttpError.

44
00:03:33,965 --> 00:03:39,903
The built-in views use the names, so for 404 a template named HttpError__404

45
00:03:39,903 --> 00:03:42,727
never replaces the built-in page.

46
00:03:43,477 --> 00:03:47,471
For each name, a template written in the admin wins over a view

47
00:03:47,471 --> 00:03:51,927
in the theme, and a view in the theme wins over the view of the module.

48
00:03:52,677 --> 00:03:56,565
A path that ends in a known file extension, such as a missing

49
00:03:56,565 --> 00:04:00,337
image, keeps its plain error status and gets no error page.

50
00:04:01,087 --> 00:04:06,110
When the site does not run in the Development environment, an unhandled exception

51
00:04:06,110 --> 00:04:11,248
goes to the Error route with no status code, and the HttpError view draws the page.

52
00:04:11,248 --> 00:04:14,307
In Development, that handler is not registered.

53
00:04:16,307 --> 00:04:19,952
Error pages are shapes, so a view in your theme or a template

54
00:04:19,952 --> 00:04:22,073
in the admin changes any of them.

55
00:04:22,073 --> 00:04:23,487
Thanks for watching.

